{"causes":["The integration of oral traditions and Buddhist teachings in Tibet created a fertile ground for the emergence of epic poetry.","The historical context of warfare and tribal conflicts in the Tibetan plateau necessitated the creation of heroic narratives to inspire and unify communities.","The patronage of local kings and lamas promoted the recording and dissemination of the King Gesar epic as part of cultural preservation efforts."],"effects":["The Epic of King Gesar has shaped Tibetan cultural identity, influencing religious practices and community values for centuries.","It has inspired numerous artistic expressions across Asia, including theater, dance, and visual arts, reinforcing its cultural significance.","The recognition of the epic by UNESCO has elevated its status, fostering interest and scholarly research in Tibetan literature and oral traditions."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"tibetan-buddhism","sourceName":"Tibetan Buddhism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"epic-of-king-gesar","targetName":"Epic of King Gesar","context":"Tibetan Buddhism has profoundly influenced the themes and moral lessons portrayed in the Epic of King Gesar."},{"sourceSlug":"mongolian-empire","sourceName":"Mongolian Empire","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"epic-of-king-gesar","targetName":"Epic of King Gesar","context":"The Mongolian Empire's cultural exchanges enriched the storytelling traditions surrounding the Epic of King Gesar."},{"sourceSlug":"tibet","sourceName":"Tibet","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"epic-of-king-gesar","targetName":"Epic of King Gesar","context":"Tibet serves as the geographical and cultural heart of the Epic of King Gesar's narrative."},{"sourceSlug":"oral-tradition","sourceName":"Oral Tradition","verb":"TRANSMITS","targetSlug":"epic-of-king-gesar","targetName":"Epic of King Gesar","context":"The epic was preserved and transmitted through generations of oral tradition before being written down in the 11th century."},{"sourceSlug":"tibetan-culture","sourceName":"Tibetan Culture","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"epic-of-king-gesar","targetName":"Epic of King Gesar","context":"The Epic of King Gesar is a defining text of Tibetan culture, encapsulating its values, beliefs, and historical experiences."}],"places":[{"name":"Lhasa, Tibet","role":"Cultural center"},{"name":"Xining, China","role":"Major recording site"},{"name":"Ulaanbaatar, Mongolia","role":"Cultural exchange hub"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Epic of King Gesar","type":"Reference article","slug":"epic-of-king-gesar"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q842370","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epic_of_King_Gesar"],"tags":["Mongolia","epic poem","Asia","Epic Poetry & Mythology","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Epic of King Gesar (Tibetan: གེ་སར་རྒྱལ་པོ་, Gesar Gyelpo; Mongolian: Гэсэрийн тууль, Geseriyn tuul; also Geser) is the world's longest surviving epic poem — with more than one million verses in its extended versions, far exceeding the combined length of Homer's Iliad and Odyssey — performed and transmitted across Tibet, Mongolia, and adjacent Himalayan and Central Asian cultures over a period of approximately a thousand years (from c. 10th–11th century CE to the present). The epic narrates the life of the divine hero-king Gesar of Ling — a supernatural warrior king sent by the divine realm to conquer the demons and enemies of the dharma, unite the land, and establish a golden age of peace and justice — and encompasses his miraculous birth, his trials and contests for kingship, his thirty-plus military campaigns against demon-kings and human enemies, and his eventual departure from the human world. The epic's core narrative combines Tibetan Buddhist cosmology (Gesar as an emanation of Padmasambhava or Avalokitesvara) with the steppe heroic tradition of the Tibetan and Mongolian worlds.

The Epic of King Gesar is a living oral tradition — performed by specialist bards (grung-mkhan in Tibetan) who claim to receive the epic through visionary experience and dreams rather than deliberate memorisation, a tradition of inspired recitation unique in world epic literature. Gesar bards may perform for days, reciting episodes spontaneously in a state of trance-like inspiration, and different regions have different versions of the epic — the Tibetan, Mongolian, Buriat, Kalmyk, and other versions all have distinctive narrative traditions. The oral tradition is still active: in Tibet, the Gesar bards continue to perform, and the epic has been partially written down in a standardised Tibetan edition running to more than 120 volumes.

The Epic of King Gesar was inscribed on UNESCO's Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2009 (jointly by China). As both the longest epic in the world and the primary monument of Tibetan and Mongolian literary and cultural tradition, it is one of the most extraordinary achievements of oral epic literature in human history.","subjects":["Tibetan Literature","Medieval Era","Oral Epic","Tibetan Buddhism","Mongolian Culture","Central Asian Literature","UNESCO Heritage","World's Longest Epic"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Epic of King Gesar significantly shaped Tibetan identity and cultural practices, with its influence extending to various artistic forms, making it a cornerstone of regional cultural heritage.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
